# ===================================================================
# REMOVED: the node-tar 6.2.1 block and sigstore CVE-2026-48815.
#
# Both were attributed to `pnpm deploy --prod` resolving old versions "despite
# pnpm.overrides". That was wrong, and the wrong attribution is what made these
# entries keep growing new CVE ids: tar 6.2.1, brace-expansion 2.0.1 and
# sigstore 2.3.1 were never in our lockfile at all. They lived in the base
# image's own bundled npm, under usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/. No override
# could ever have reached them.
#
# apps/api/Dockerfile now upgrades that npm to 11, which brings tar 7.x,
# a patched brace-expansion and sigstore 4.x, so these findings are gone rather
# than silenced. If a future Trivy finding names a package at a version the
# lockfile does not contain, check `usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/` and any
# dependency's `dist/compiled/` before adding an entry here.
# ===================================================================

# ===================================================================
# brace-expansion CVE-2026-14257 (HIGH, = GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg) — DoS via
# unbounded brace expansion, affects 1.1.16 and 2.1.2. No patched release
# exists on either line; the fix ships only in 5.0.8. Forcing everything to
# >=5.0.8 resolves cleanly and then breaks the toolchain (5.x drops the
# callable CJS default export, so minimatch@3.1.5 under eslint 9 throws
# "expand is not a function" and all three lint:* scripts die). 1.x/2.x reach
# us only via minimatch@3/@9, whose patterns come from our own configs and
# never from user input, so the DoS is not reachable.
#
# This is the SAME exemption as pnpm.auditConfig.ignoreGhsas, documented in
# docs/SECURITY_DEPS.md. Both gates scan the lockfile and both report it, so
# an exemption has to be recorded in BOTH places or one gate stays red.
# Remove from here and from ignoreGhsas together.
# ===================================================================
CVE-2026-14257

# ===================================================================
# brace-expansion CVE-2026-69152 (HIGH, = GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895) — the follow-up
# that bypasses the CVE-2026-14257 mitigation above. Fixed in 1.1.18 / 2.1.4 /
# 3.0.6 / 5.0.9, and our lockfile carries all of those: pnpm.overrides was
# raised to exactly these in #535, and osv-scanner reports 0 unignored
# high/critical against pnpm-lock.yaml.
#
# The copy Trivy finds is not ours. It is npm's own bundled dependency at
# usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion/package.json,
# version 5.0.7 — the case the note above says to check for first. No override
# reaches inside npm, and unlike the tar/sigstore findings that this file used
# to carry, upgrading npm does not clear it either. Verified by unpacking the
# published tarballs rather than assuming:
#
#     npm@11.19.0 (latest 11.x, what the Dockerfile installs) → 5.0.7
#     npm@12.0.2  (latest, needs a newer Node than this base) → 5.0.7
#
# So there is no npm release to move to yet. The vulnerability is a DoS via
# unbounded brace expansion, reachable only by expanding an attacker-chosen
# pattern; npm in the runtime image never installs anything (see the comment
# on the `npm install -g npm@11` line in apps/api/Dockerfile — it is kept only
# because migrate.sh shells out to `npx prisma migrate deploy`), so no
# attacker-controlled pattern reaches it.
#
# Not added to pnpm.auditConfig.ignoreGhsas, unlike CVE-2026-14257 above: that
# one is in the lockfile so both gates report it. This one is not, so the
# lockfile gate is already clean and an entry there would be dead config.
#
# REMOVE when npm ships a bundled brace-expansion >= 5.0.9. Re-check with:
#     npm pack npm@11 && tar -xzOf npm-*.tgz \
#       package/node_modules/brace-expansion/package.json | grep version
# ===================================================================
CVE-2026-69152

# ===================================================================
# ip-address CVE-2026-69192 (HIGH, = GHSA-mwp4-54f8-5fhr) — Address4 decodes
# leading-zero octets as decimal while resolvers decode them as octal, so a
# host like 0177.0.0.1 can pass an allowlist and then resolve somewhere else.
# Fixed in 10.3.1.
#
# Same shape as the entry above, and the same non-fix: our lockfile is on
# 10.4.0 (pnpm.overrides pins `ip-address@<10.3.1` → `>=10.3.1 <11`, added in
# #535). The 10.2.0 Trivy sees is npm's bundled copy at
# usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/ip-address/package.json, reached
# through its socks-proxy-agent, and no override goes inside npm.
#
# Not reachable here regardless: it is an SSRF/allowlist-bypass primitive, and
# it would have to be npm parsing an attacker-supplied proxy host. npm in the
# runtime image never installs anything and no proxy is configured.
#
# Added in the same pass as CVE-2026-69152 but NOT reported by the same CI run
# — the 02:24 build on b9ec4891 saw only the brace-expansion one. This landed
# in Trivy's database within the hour. If Docker Build is red on a base-image
# CVE, rescan locally before assuming the CI list is complete:
#
#     docker build -t repro - <<'EOF'
#     FROM node:20-alpine
#     RUN apk add --no-cache --upgrade openssl libcrypto3 libssl3
#     RUN npm install -g npm@11
#     EOF
#     docker run --rm -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
#       -v "$PWD/.trivyignore:/w/.trivyignore:ro" -w /w \
#       aquasec/trivy:latest image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --scanners vuln repro
#
# REMOVE when npm ships a bundled ip-address >= 10.3.1.
# ===================================================================
CVE-2026-69192

# ===================================================================
# image-size CVE-2025-71329 (= GHSA-5p2g-fcmc-qvqq, JXL/HEIF) and
# CVE-2025-71330 (= GHSA-w3rx-r6r6-pgpr, ICNS) — both HIGH, both infinite
# loops in a format parser (DoS).
#
# Unlike the two entries above, this one IS in our lockfile (image-size@1.2.1),
# so both gates report it and the exemption is recorded in BOTH places:
# pnpm.auditConfig.ignoreGhsas + docs/SECURITY_DEPS.md carry the GHSA ids,
# these two lines carry the CVE ids. Remove them together.
#
# There is nothing to pin to. Both advisories read `introduced: 0 …
# last_affected: 2.0.2`, and 2.0.2 is what npm serves as `latest` (`legacy` is
# our 1.2.1) — checked against the registry, not assumed. Every published
# version is affected.
#
# Sole consumer is metro@0.83.3, the React Native bundler: it reads image
# dimensions off the repo's own committed assets during a mobile build. It is
# a devDependency of apps/mobile, ships in no deployed image (apps/api declares
# no metro/expo dependency), and the attacker-supplied image this needs never
# reaches that path.
#
# REMOVE when image-size publishes above 2.0.2 — then pin it, drop these two
# lines and the two GHSAs from pnpm.auditConfig.ignoreGhsas, and confirm
# `tsx scripts/check-dependency-audit.ts` stays green.
# ===================================================================
CVE-2025-71329
CVE-2025-71330

# extract-zip@2.0.1 (= GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv). No patched release exists as of
# 2026-08-12. It is reachable only through Puppeteer's vendor-browser installer,
# not through the deployed API or any user-supplied archive path. Remove this
# together with the pnpm audit exemption when extract-zip or Puppeteer ships a fix.
CVE-2026-56876
